WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:12.720
[Anita Schorr] I was living, uh, with my family,

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:13.960
my father, mother,

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:17.040
and a brother, who was five years younger.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.660
I went to school. We were living a life of
wonderful

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:24.760
affluence and happiness.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.099
We went skiing in the winter,

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.020
swimming in the summer.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:33.260
This is, uh, my brother and myself.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:39.620
My Jewishness was mo—mostly

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.340
combined with—with sports.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.660
And we—we went Friday night,

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:46.550
sometimes, into the synagogue.

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:49.380
But it was more of an Oneg Shabbat kind of thing.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.780
I know we drove with the car to the synagogue.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.560
You lost your childhood, I think, at that
moment.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.760
You suddenly were wearing a star,

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.540
and you were not allowed to be in a tram,

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.000
sitting with other people.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.080
You had to be standing on—on one of the
outside places.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:14.880
Very similar to discrimination in this country,

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.480
where the Blacks were pushed to the side.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.320
In Theresienstadt, at one point,

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.320
they were testing some, um,

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.240
vaccine against scarlet fever.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.070
So people were sick.

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:36.980
Then they were trying to get some vaccine
for jaundice.

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:39.399
I was laying in bed with my brother

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.400
that had an enormous jaundice.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:43.700
And my mother had

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.620
very dangerous scarlet fever.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.940
She was burning up with enormous fever.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:58.700
It was freezing. It was muddy.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.040
They didn't let people go to the bathroom.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.500
And people had to relieve themselves

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:08.200
while they were standing with tears in their eyes.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.660
I remember one incident, my brother, uh,

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:14.940
telling my mother that he was hungry.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.620
And she had nothing to give him. And she was
crying. And—

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:23.480
that was certainly enough for
me never to mention anything,

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:24.680
anything at all.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:39.840
My mother said—now I was, at that point,
almost 14—

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.460
she said, “You are going to tell them you
are 18.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:46.020
And you volunteer. And you go, and you—

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:49.480
you go with the rest of the women.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.190
I will stay with my brother.”

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:54.880
And my—her explanation was

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.080
that she has to stay with my brother,

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:00.160
because he would forget his name.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.060
And I must admit that

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:05.810
I was very angry at my mother for

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:08.940
a very, very long time,

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:12.400
that she didn't save herself for me.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:15.320
I passed through the selection.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:19.660
I was now selected to go to Germany to work.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.640
And I suddenly see that there was somebody—

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:24.760
they were examining women.

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:27.180
And somebody got up from the table.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.640
And she was bleeding.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.280
And I lost it. I started to cry,

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:32.980
“I want back to my mommy.”

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:35.380
And they couldn't quiet me down.

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:39.800
And a—and a German lady, a guard, 
came over to me

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.800
and asked me, “Why are you crying?”

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.440
And I spoke very nicely German.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:48.140
And I said, “I want to go back to my mommy.”

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:50.760
She took me to her room,

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.400
and she gave me cocoa,

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:57.440
which I didn't see for years, 
and years, and years.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:59.240
And she says to me, “Now,

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:00.980
I will tell you something.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:03.540
And please, listen to me.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:05.220
Don't cry,

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.340
and don't try to get back to your parents.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:10.670
You are better off. You are here with women

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:14.280
that know you. They speak the same language.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.340
And you're going out into Germany.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:19.140
You're gonna—you're getting out of here.”

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:23.500
She really saved my life.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:27.760
And then I found out later

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:30.540
that my mother, with my little brother,

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.560
they went to the gas chambers

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:34.540
the next day.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.980
The moment we left...

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:39.220
The—the men left.

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:42.720
Then the women left for hard labor to go to Germany.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:46.220
They took the older and the women with the children,

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:47.240
and they gassed them.

